<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tagma</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as tagmata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The tagmata were the elite regiments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>'s central army in the 8th to 11th centuries; from the 4th century the Greek word tagma had meant an infantry battalion of 200–400 men. After putting down the revolt of Artabasdos in 741–743, Emperor Constantine V reformed the old guard units of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> into professional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> regiments, garrisoned around the capital as a central reserve. In the 9th and early 10th centuries there were four: the Scholai, the Excubitors, the Vigla and the Hikanatoi. New units followed, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a> of about 988; the older tagmata declined after about 1050 and had mostly vanished by about 1100.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tagma-military.jpg" alt="Tagma" width="330" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, 13th-century author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fighting_between_Byzantines_and_Arabs_Chronikon_of_Ioannis_Skylitzes,_end_of_13th_century..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Beside the provincial soldiers stood the professional regiments (<b>tagmata</b>) of Constantinople and, from about 988, the Varangian Guard.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
